<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Emirate of Sicily</h1><p class="st-meta">831 CE – 1091 CE (approx.) · capital Palermo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Muslim Sicily, ruled from Palermo for over two centuries, which transformed the island's farming, cities and language before the Norman conquest.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 827 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabids of Ifriqiya</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> at the invitation of the rebel Byzantine commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphemius">Euphemius</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> fell in 831 and became the capital, but the conquest was slow: <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syracuse">Syracuse</a> fell in 878, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taormina">Taormina</a> in 902 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rometta">Rometta</a> in 965. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a> was taken in 870.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After Fatimid takeover in 909, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalbid-dynasty">Kalbid</a> family ruled as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> from 948, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-al-kalbi">Hasan al-Kalbi</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> grew into one of the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> cities; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hawqal">Ibn Hawqal</a> counted some 300 mosques. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> introduced citrus, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and new irrigation techniques. Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> lived as protected subjects, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siculo-arabic">Siculo-Arabic</a>, the ancestor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maltese-language">Maltese</a>, spread widely.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the 1040s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> split among rival lords. One of them, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-al-thumna">Ibn al-Thumna</a>, invited the Normans, and Roger I took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a> in 1061 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> in 1072; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noto-sicily">Noto</a>, the last Muslim stronghold, fell in 1091. Arab-Norman culture flourished under the Norman kings.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/emirate-of-sicily.jpg" alt="Emirate of Sicily" width="320" height="255"><figcaption>Image: MrsColdArrow · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Islamic_Sicily_under_the_Kalbid_Dynasty_c.1000_AD.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>831 CE – 1091 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Palermo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hasan al-Kalbi, Abu al-Futuh Yusuf</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>, <a href="/countries/malta">Malta</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Islamic Sicily, Kalbid Emirate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=961&amp;focus=emirate-of-sicily" aria-label="Emirate of Sicily on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5013 808 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Emirate of Sicily lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5333 1064L5255 1145M5333 1064L5383 1099M5333 1064L5811 1337M5333 1064L5333 1064"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5255 1145h0M5383 1099h0M5811 1337h0M5333 1064h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5333 1064h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5333 1064h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Palermo. <a href